Project: BodyBrain- Tagline: A living memory of your body, from everyday activity to clinical evidence.
- One-line idea: BodyBrain is a personal AI agent that continuously builds a searchable, visual memory of your body by connecting medical imaging, reports, doctor conversations, prescriptions, treatments, injuries, workouts, wearable data, symptoms, and recovery to an interactive 3D anatomical model.
- Core promise: BodyBrain remembers what happened, where it happened, when it happened, what evidence supports it, what was done about it, and how your body changed afterward.
- Personal Agent angle: The product is not simply a medical chatbot, fitness tracker, document search engine, or anatomy viewer. The product is the persistent memory layer connecting all of those systems.
- 1. The problem BodyBrain solves
  - Your medical and physical history is fragmented.
  - An MRI may live in a hospital portal.
  - The radiology report may be a PDF.
  - A doctor may verbally explain something important during an appointment.
  - Prescriptions live somewhere else.
  - Physiotherapy instructions may be on paper.
  - Running data may be in Strava.
  - Gym routines may be in another application.
  - Heart rate, sleep, steps, and recovery information may be inside a wearable application.
  - Pain or symptoms may exist only in your memory.
  - Months later, it becomes difficult to reconstruct the complete story.
  - BodyBrain turns all of those disconnected records into one longitudinal memory of the person's body.
- 2. The central concept
  - Imagine opening BodyBrain and seeing a 3D representation of the human body.
  - Every anatomical structure can become a memory node.
  - Clicking the spine could reveal:
    - MRI findings
    - X-rays
    - Doctor visits
    - Pain history
    - Medications
    - Physiotherapy
    - Gym exercises
    - Running activity
    - Previous injuries
    - Recovery progress
  - Clicking a knee, shoulder, nerve, muscle, organ, joint, or vertebral level does the same thing.
  - The 3D body therefore becomes a visual index into the user's personal history.
