- 4. Medical imaging is a first-class part of BodyBrain
  - The imaging layer should be one of the product's strongest features.
  - A user can upload or import:
    - MRI studies
    - CT studies
    - X-rays
    - Ultrasound
    - Other supported imaging
    - Radiology reports
  - BodyBrain separates:
    - The actual imaging study
    - The radiologist's report
    - The doctor's interpretation
    - The resulting diagnosis
    - Treatment decisions
    - Prescriptions
    - Follow-up findings
  - This distinction also aligns naturally with healthcare-data models such as FHIR, where DiagnosticReport can reference the corresponding ImagingStudy. HL7
- 5. Anatomical precision
  - BodyBrain should map information to the most specific anatomical structure actually supported by the record.
  - Example:
    - Poor output: Back problem
    - Better output: Lumbar spine
    - Better: L5-S1
    - Better: L5-S1 intervertebral disc
    - More precise when documented: Right S1 nerve root
  - Another example:
    - Shoulder
    - → Rotator cuff
    - → Supraspinatus tendon
    - → Partial-thickness tear
  - Another:
    - Knee
    - → Medial meniscus
    - → Posterior horn
    - → documented finding
  - The system should never invent anatomical specificity that the medical record does not provide.
- 6. Evidence-first design
  - Every important finding should have a provenance trail.
  - If BodyBrain highlights the right S1 nerve root, the user should be able to click Why?
  - BodyBrain then shows:
    - Source document
    - Date
    - Report type
    - Original finding
    - Doctor discussion
    - Related treatment
    - Follow-up evidence
  - This is one of the most important features in the entire product.
  - BodyBrain should distinguish:
    - Explicitly stated in radiology report
    - Documented by physician
    - Extracted from medical record
    - Reported by patient
    - Derived anatomical mapping
    - Imported from wearable
  - Original clinical wording should always remain accessible.
- 7. Example clinical memory
  - Suppose an MRI says:
    - L5-S1 disc protrusion with right S1 nerve root involvement
  - BodyBrain creates connected memories:
    - Lumbar spine
    - → L5-S1
    - → Disc protrusion
    - → Right S1 nerve root
    - → MRI
    - → Radiology report
    - → Doctor appointment
    - → Leg symptoms
    - → Prescription
    - → Physiotherapy
    - → Recovery
  - Months later, the user can ask:
    - "Which nerve was affected?"
    - "What did my MRI actually say?"
    - "When was this discovered?"
    - "What did the doctor recommend?"
    - "Why was I prescribed this medicine?"
    - "How did my activity change afterward?"
- 8. The 3D Body Memory Atlas
  - The 3D human model remains the primary interface.
  - The Human Atlas project is particularly useful as a prototype foundation because its current viewer exposes more than 2,000 selectable anatomical meshes, system filtering, search, isolation, and interactive selection using React and Three.js. GitHub
  - BodyBrain adds a personal-memory layer on top of that anatomy.
  - Possible visual states:
    - Normal anatomy
    - Structures containing memories
    - Structures with recent activity
    - Structures with documented medical findings
    - Structures currently selected
  - Selecting a structure opens:
    - Clinical evidence
    - Timeline
    - Related documents
    - Symptoms
    - Exercises
    - Injuries
    - Treatments
    - Agent conversation
- 9. Important limitation for exact nerve visualization
  - The current Human Atlas model is excellent as the general body interface, but exact peripheral nerve coverage should not be assumed for every nerve the product eventually needs.
  - The repository currently uses a BodyParts3D-based model with 2,234 selectable meshes, and a separate peripheral-nerve expansion has appeared as an open contribution rather than something to assume is already part of the main product. GitHub
  - Therefore the architecture should allow additional anatomical models or nerve overlays later.
  - For the hackathon, choose one anatomical area that the available model represents well.
- 10. Medical Document to Interactive Body
  - The user uploads:
    - MRI report
    - X-ray report
    - Prescription
    - Visit summary
    - Physiotherapy report
  - BodyBrain performs:
    - Text extraction
    - Document classification
    - Date extraction
    - Anatomical entity extraction
    - Laterality extraction
    - Finding extraction
    - Condition extraction
    - Medication extraction
    - Procedure extraction
    - Clinician extraction
    - Relationship extraction
  - It then maps those entities to the body.
  - Example input:
    - "Moderate right neural foraminal stenosis at C5-C6."
  - Generated structured memory:
    - Region: cervical spine
    - Level: C5-C6
    - Side: right
    - Structure: neural foramen
    - Finding: stenosis
    - Source: MRI report
    - Date: report date
  - The record itself remains attached.
- 11. Doctor Discussion Memory
  - Medical history is more than reports.
  - BodyBrain should also remember what happened during appointments.
  - A user could:
    - Upload visit notes
    - Import summaries
    - Type what the doctor said
    - Eventually import transcripts where permitted
  - Extract:
    - Doctor's explanation
    - Diagnosis discussed
    - Alternative possibilities
    - Treatment recommendations
    - Restrictions
    - Follow-up requirements
    - Referral information
    - Medication changes
    - Questions asked
    - Questions left unanswered
  - This makes the personal agent much more useful than a static medical-record viewer.
- 12. Prescription Memory
  - A medication should never appear as an isolated name.
  - BodyBrain links it to its clinical context.
  - Example:
    - Medication
    - → prescribed during → May 14 orthopedic appointment
    - → associated with → right-leg nerve pain
    - → following → MRI
    - → related anatomy → lumbar spine
    - → stopped/changed → June 10
  - This lets the user ask:
    - "Why was this prescribed?"
    - "When did I start it?"
    - "What issue was it connected to?"
    - "Did anything change after I started it?"
- 13. Injury and Recovery Brain
  - Every injury becomes a persistent object.
  - It contains:
    - Initial symptoms
    - Injury date
    - Related activities
    - Medical visits
