- 34. Hackathon demo
  - Start with the 3D body.
  - Say:
    - "Most AI assistants know a lot about medicine, but they know almost nothing about what has happened to your body."
  - Upload an MRI report.
  - BodyBrain extracts the anatomical finding.
  - Relevant anatomy highlights.
  - Open doctor notes.
  - Show treatment and prescription.
  - Show Strava/gym context before and after the event.
  - Ask:
    - "Tell me the complete story of this problem."
  - Agent produces chronological history.
  - Ask:
    - "Which nerve was involved, and why are you saying that?"
  - Agent opens the supporting report.
  - Finally:
    - "Prepare this for my new doctor."
  - Generate the patient-controlled medical history.
- 35. The killer feature
  - The strongest feature is not simply:
    - AI
    - 3D anatomy
    - MRI parsing
    - Strava
    - RAG
  - It is:
    - BodyBrain can explain its personal memory through evidence.
  - "Why do you think my S1 nerve was involved?"
  - The answer should come from the user's actual medical history, not generic model knowledge.
- 36. The strongest differentiation
  - A medical portal stores records.
  - A PACS viewer shows scans.
  - Strava remembers activities.
  - Wearables remember metrics.
  - Gym apps remember workouts.
  - Notes remember conversations.
  - AI chatbots answer questions.
  - BodyBrain remembers how all of those things relate to the same human body over time.
- 37. What BodyBrain should not claim
  - It should not present an AI inference as though a radiologist or physician documented it.
  - It should distinguish between:
    - Record fact
    - Patient report
    - Extracted structured information
    - AI anatomical mapping
  - That distinction actually makes the demo stronger because the user can trust where information came from.
